Is the native decoder actually using NEON?
==========================================

Date     2026-10-31
Commit   60e69f57d (plus the NEON guard added in the commit that carries this log)
Subject  libzxingnative.so, zxing-cpp v3.1.1, NDK 29.0.14206865, CMake RelWithDebInfo
Why      Phase 5 item 7 names "zxing-cpp + ARM NEON". "NEON" was asserted in the plan
         and never checked. This checks it.

Finding 1 -- zxing-cpp contains no NEON intrinsics at all
----------------------------------------------------------
There is no arm_neon.h, no vld1/vmlaq, no hand-written SIMD kernel anywhere in
core/src. The only mention of the architecture is a feature test:

  core/src/GlobalHistogramBinarizer.cpp:140
    #if defined(__AVX__) || defined(__ARM_NEON)
      ... copy a strided column into a packed buffer first ...
    #endif

and its own comment explains why: the copy is worth paying for because "the
auto-vectorizer makes that part 8x faster", not because of intrinsics.

So "zxing-cpp + NEON" does not mean a hand-vectorised decoder. It means two things
that have to hold:

  a) __ARM_NEON must be defined, or that packed-copy path is compiled out silently;
  b) the auto-vectorizer must actually emit SIMD.

Both are checked below. Neither was checked before.

Finding 2 -- (a) holds, and is now enforced at build time
----------------------------------------------------------
zxing_native.cpp now fails to compile on an ARM ABI if __ARM_NEON is undefined.
It shares toolchain flags with zxing-cpp's translation units, since zxing-cpp is
added to the same CMake project.

Confirmed on the device as well, through NativeFrameDecoder.buildInfo(), which
reports compile-time facts rather than a runtime CPU probe:

  abi=arm64-v8a;neon=1;zxingcpp=3.1.1

An attempt to defeat it through the documented switch failed, which is itself worth
recording:

  -DANDROID_ARM_NEON=FALSE
  => CMake Error at .../android-legacy.toolchain.cmake:171
     "Disabling Neon is no longer supported"

NDK 29 no longer allows NEON to be turned off for armeabi-v7a. So the guard cannot
fire through that route today. It was verified to have teeth by the route that is
still open -- a stray -mfpu flag, one line away in any build file:

  cppFlags += "-mfpu=vfpv3-d16"   (armeabi-v7a)
  => zxing_native.cpp:37: error: "__ARM_NEON is not defined for this ARM ABI ..."

Finding 3 -- (b) holds: the auto-vectorizer did emit SIMD, on both shipped ABIs
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Instruction census over the stripped release libraries, llvm-objdump -d:

  arm64-v8a
    instructions total                                  260646
    using vector registers (v0.16b / .8h / .4s / .2d)   7089

  armeabi-v7a
    instructions total                                  271472
    using q registers (NEON-only, never scalar VFP)     1281
    vld1 / vst1 (NEON element load/store)               3033

The armeabi-v7a count is deliberately restricted to q-register operands and
vld1/vst1. A naive count of "v*" mnemonics on ARMv7 is misleading, because vmov,
vadd and friends are also scalar VFP instructions and would inflate the number.

What this does NOT say
-----------------------
- Nothing about speed. This is a census of instructions in a binary, not a
  measurement. Whether any of it makes a frame decode faster is phase 5 item 10's
  A/B, under the gates of BENCHMARKS.md section 10.6, and no claim may be made
  before that.
- Nothing about WHICH functions were vectorised. The census is library-wide. The
  hot path for this app is the binarizer and the 1D/2D locators; whether the
  vectorised instructions are in those or in unrelated code is not established
  here.
- Nothing about armeabi-v7a devices in particular. No such device is available,
  so that ABI is built and inspected but has never been run.
